# Documentation prose (no AI tells)

When writing or editing docs (Markdown, guides, READMEs, OVERRIDES, drafts):

## Punctuation (ASCII only)

- Use ASCII hyphen `-` only. Never em dash `—` or en dash `–`.
- Prefer commas, periods, colons, or parentheses over dash asides.
- Use straight quotes `"` and `'` only. Never curly `“ ” ‘ ’`.
- Use `...` not the ellipsis character `…`.
- Prefer `A16` / `A13-A16` over typographic range dashes.

```markdown
<!-- BAD -->
Bass extends Android — it does not replace it.
“just install AOSP”

<!-- GOOD -->
Bass extends Android; it does not replace it.
"just install AOSP"
```

Title pattern: `Bass - High Level Overview` (spaces around `-`), not `Bass — …`.

## Phrasing to avoid

Do not paste stock LLM filler:

- "In today's rapidly evolving…", "In the world of…", "When it comes to…"
- "delve", "landscape", "robust", "seamless", "leverage", "utilize", "empower"
- "It's important to note that", "It is worth noting", "At the end of the day"
- Triple negation punchlines: "Not X. Not Y. It is Z."
- Fake enthusiasm, emoji decoration, or marketing glitter unless the existing page already uses that voice

## Voice

- Match the surrounding doc: plain, concrete, product-name accurate.
- Prefer short sentences and tables over ornate parallel lists.
- Keep Bliss/Bass naming rules from the Product Guide (public product names, no internal codenames in customer-facing text).

Before finishing a docs edit, search the touched files for `—`, `–`, `“`, `”`, `‘`, `’`, and `…` and replace them.
